1
00:00:04,307 --> 00:00:15,389
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to continue that sutta we spoke about last time, we've only got to the third perception that needs to be developed.

2
00:00:16,049 --> 00:00:17,089
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is the fourth one.

3
00:00:18,569 --> 00:00:32,932
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, when a bhikkhu becomes accustomed to the perception of non-delight in regard to the whole world, their mind draws back from the various beautiful things in the world, and either equanimity or

4
00:00:34,873 --> 00:00:37,175
[SPEAKER_00]: The other side gets established.

5
00:00:37,835 --> 00:00:38,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty cool.

6
00:00:38,375 --> 00:00:39,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

7
00:00:39,536 --> 00:00:49,823
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we said in the beginning, the first one was the beautiful side of the ugly, the living side of death, and the necessity of food.

8
00:00:50,044 --> 00:00:51,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, right, right.

9
00:00:51,985 --> 00:00:54,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the fact that food is the necessity.

10
00:00:54,627 --> 00:00:56,808
[SPEAKER_01]: You're subjected to it and dependent on it.

11
00:00:56,988 --> 00:00:57,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

12
00:00:57,309 --> 00:00:58,349
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no choice about it.

13
00:00:58,750 --> 00:00:59,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

14
00:00:59,370 --> 00:01:00,571
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in itself, it's a burden.

15
00:01:01,351 --> 00:01:01,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

16
00:01:04,725 --> 00:01:21,100
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is saying, the perception of non-delight... Well, it's to uproot the inherent delight, joy, in regard to the world, in regard to the senses, in regard to the sense objects.

17
00:01:22,862 --> 00:01:23,883
[SPEAKER_00]: By developing what?

18
00:01:24,524 --> 00:01:26,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, by developing the non-delight.

19
00:01:27,246 --> 00:01:30,009
[SPEAKER_01]: And you develop the non-delight by seeing the danger of delight.

20
00:01:31,897 --> 00:01:44,685
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for example, seeing things you delight in, like everybody... and, you know, delight, the translation... the word delight is not... it's not like you're... you're over the moon ecstatic and delighted.

21
00:01:44,745 --> 00:01:54,871
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... it's... it refers to that subtle attitude of acceptance, of looking forward to, on a mental level, towards things that are pleasing and agreeable.

22
00:01:55,432 --> 00:01:56,072
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you delight.

23
00:01:56,112 --> 00:01:56,732
[SPEAKER_01]: You're related.

24
00:01:57,113 --> 00:02:00,755
[SPEAKER_01]: On that mental level, there is joy at the prospect of something pleasant.

25
00:02:02,755 --> 00:02:12,079
[SPEAKER_01]: So, things that are pleasant are not problem in themselves, but it's you being unable to not delight in the prospect of pleasure.

26
00:02:12,159 --> 00:02:13,100
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the problem is.

27
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:17,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Because delighting in it means not seeing the inherent danger.

28
00:02:20,042 --> 00:02:26,705
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're seeing the inherent danger in things you are delighting towards, you stop delighting.

29
00:02:27,994 --> 00:02:37,358
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not going to blame those things or, like, try to never see anything agreeable or hear anything agreeable ever again.

30
00:02:38,199 --> 00:02:39,880
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that you will not delight in it.

31
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:43,381
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if it's agreeable, it's known as agreeable, but there's no delight.

32
00:02:43,782 --> 00:02:45,923
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's disagreeable, it's known as disagreeable.

33
00:02:45,983 --> 00:02:50,305
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no, like, resistance to it, which is the opposite of that delight.

34
00:02:51,185 --> 00:02:53,706
[SPEAKER_01]: So, non-delight refers to the absence of delight.

35
00:02:53,946 --> 00:02:56,527
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't refer to hating that which you delight in.

36
00:02:56,947 --> 00:03:01,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it's no longer attracted to the beautiful things in the world.

37
00:03:01,468 --> 00:03:02,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you are not attracted.

38
00:03:02,908 --> 00:03:05,469
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean the beautiful things in the world cease to exist.

39
00:03:05,909 --> 00:03:07,670
[SPEAKER_01]: It just means you don't delight in it.

40
00:03:08,330 --> 00:03:12,272
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't just fabricate non-delight out of thin air.

41
00:03:12,692 --> 00:03:14,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, now I'll just practice non-delight.

42
00:03:14,513 --> 00:03:17,814
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only practice non-delight by preventing yourself from delighting in it.

43
00:03:17,854 --> 00:03:25,578
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only way you can prevent yourself from delighting in it is by contemplating, maintaining the context of the danger in things that you are delighting in.

44
00:03:27,653 --> 00:03:29,254
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cultivate non-delights then.

45
00:03:29,814 --> 00:03:36,357
[SPEAKER_01]: And you do that long enough, then you don't have to constantly keep reminding yourself of the danger, because the danger is inherent in those things.

46
00:03:36,437 --> 00:03:39,098
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why a freedom from those things is possible in the first place.

47
00:03:40,719 --> 00:03:46,622
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not seeing the danger, you build up the delight, so to speak, in the course of lifetime... well, lifetimes.

48
00:03:51,073 --> 00:03:54,195
[SPEAKER_00]: You get taken by the... like with food, you get taken with the tastes.

49
00:03:55,236 --> 00:03:55,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

50
00:03:56,076 --> 00:03:58,438
[SPEAKER_01]: The taste is the... yeah, the taste is the measure.

51
00:03:58,458 --> 00:03:59,258
[SPEAKER_01]: The taste is your motive.

52
00:03:59,278 --> 00:04:01,660
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where your motivation is rooted.

53
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:12,146
[SPEAKER_01]: And yeah, the food is a good example, because here you can... you can abandon things that are non-essential, like agreeable sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, you restrain from, and you can say no to all of it.

54
00:04:12,927 --> 00:04:15,148
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, delight in them is bad.

55
00:04:15,308 --> 00:04:15,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

56
00:04:16,449 --> 00:04:17,290
[SPEAKER_01]: But that still doesn't mean

57
00:04:18,995 --> 00:04:24,958
[SPEAKER_01]: You understood the light simply by saying no to these things, because there are certain things you can't say no to, such as eating.

58
00:04:26,559 --> 00:04:35,683
[SPEAKER_01]: And yes, even the Buddha himself tried to starve himself to death, to see if that would be the way out of complete freedom of the mind, and then he saw actually it isn't.

59
00:04:37,824 --> 00:04:39,425
[SPEAKER_00]: It was being done for the wrong reasons.

60
00:04:39,625 --> 00:04:40,806
[SPEAKER_00]: The food was never the problem.

61
00:04:43,011 --> 00:04:54,239
[SPEAKER_01]: So, things that you can't just say no to, such as food, that's where you have to then practice developing of that context of danger in delighting in it.

62
00:04:54,439 --> 00:04:58,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the food I'm eating is dangerous, but my delight is the danger.

63
00:04:58,822 --> 00:05:04,986
[SPEAKER_01]: My delight is that which keeps me bound to this aimless wanderings of saṃsāra for eons and eons of time.

64
00:05:05,086 --> 00:05:06,527
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger of delight.

65
00:05:06,687 --> 00:05:07,628
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger of delight.

66
00:05:07,768 --> 00:05:08,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the danger of food.

67
00:05:09,249 --> 00:05:10,690
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger of delight in food.

68
00:05:12,278 --> 00:05:16,822
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if there is no delight in it, then, yeah, you wouldn't care what you're eating, good or bad.

69
00:05:17,102 --> 00:05:18,364
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what the Buddha said himself.

70
00:05:18,404 --> 00:05:23,108
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what, I think, Madhyamaka, in that manner, praised him.

71
00:05:23,148 --> 00:05:26,371
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, what do you see worthy in me of praise?

72
00:05:26,411 --> 00:05:27,992
[SPEAKER_01]: On account of what are you praising me, basically?

73
00:05:28,633 --> 00:05:32,396
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, oh, Master Gautama is one who eats little, praises eating little.

74
00:05:32,636 --> 00:05:33,897
[SPEAKER_01]: And he has disciples that eat little.

75
00:05:34,698 --> 00:05:36,800
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, he missed all these ascetic practices.

76
00:05:36,860 --> 00:05:38,542
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, that's absolutely not true.

77
00:05:39,524 --> 00:05:40,004
[SPEAKER_01]: I have...

78
00:05:40,024 --> 00:05:58,919
[SPEAKER_01]: I have disciples that sometimes don't eat for two or three days, and yet, sometimes, I would eat... kings and queens would serve me, like, the most refined food you can imagine... manage with their own hands, and I would eat a bowl full to the brim, sometimes two bowls full to the brim.

79
00:06:00,460 --> 00:06:05,144
[SPEAKER_01]: If they were to respect me on account of eating little, they wouldn't, because I don't always eat little.

80
00:06:07,490 --> 00:06:18,897
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he debunks every ascetic practice in itself as a word of respect and says, no, why they respect me is because I've shown them the path out of suffering, I've shown them... and then all these ways of practicing the dhamma that they understood for themselves.

81
00:06:18,937 --> 00:06:23,080
[SPEAKER_01]: So they know important is important and non-important is not important.

82
00:06:23,440 --> 00:06:26,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Like blaming the food and so on, it's not important.

83
00:06:26,402 --> 00:06:27,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Your delight is important.

84
00:06:28,423 --> 00:06:29,764
[SPEAKER_01]: You understand the way out of delight?

85
00:06:30,244 --> 00:06:36,827
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't care if the person who taught you the way out of delight is eating six bowls of food, because you understood what he meant.

86
00:06:36,887 --> 00:06:50,912
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if that person say you learned it from somebody... say you learned it from Sotāpanna, the way out of suffering, and you're practicing it, and you see it in them, that they are not necessarily practicing it.

87
00:06:51,312 --> 00:06:52,352
[SPEAKER_01]: They are eating carelessly.

88
00:06:53,893 --> 00:06:56,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Even that, you wouldn't hold against them, so to speak.

89
00:06:57,094 --> 00:06:59,115
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, yeah, it's whatever they do, it's on them.

90
00:07:00,393 --> 00:07:06,815
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever they do, does not invalidate the truth of the dhamma, the truth of the instruction that that person has understood.

91
00:07:07,215 --> 00:07:11,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, the Buddha himself did not eat all this food out of carelessness.

92
00:07:11,236 --> 00:07:13,597
[SPEAKER_01]: He ate because it was necessary to eat, because he was hungry.

93
00:07:14,337 --> 00:07:16,058
[SPEAKER_01]: The hunger was determining how much he'll eat.

94
00:07:17,218 --> 00:07:20,839
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't seek out kings and queens to serve him food and rejecting law or... No.

95
00:07:20,979 --> 00:07:23,700
[SPEAKER_01]: It just happened to be in such environment, such circumstances.

96
00:07:24,403 --> 00:07:32,927
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is that if you judge a book by its cover, you will put the cover first and the message of the book second.

97
00:07:33,647 --> 00:07:34,707
[SPEAKER_01]: But it should be the other way around.

98
00:07:35,068 --> 00:07:49,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if the cover of the book deteriorates and doesn't necessarily uphold the greatest standard of ascetic practice, is the message basically impenetrable to criticism, the message of freedom from suffering?

99
00:07:49,734 --> 00:07:51,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Is the principle behind freedom from suffering

100
00:07:52,533 --> 00:07:53,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely true universally.

101
00:07:54,114 --> 00:07:54,334
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

102
00:07:54,494 --> 00:07:55,135
[SPEAKER_01]: You understood it.

103
00:07:55,255 --> 00:07:55,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

104
00:07:55,835 --> 00:07:57,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter if the whole book gets destroyed.

105
00:07:58,617 --> 00:08:04,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter what what a person who told you that do or like in later in the life and so on.

106
00:08:04,801 --> 00:08:07,442
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is that the message you got, that's the truth.

107
00:08:07,462 --> 00:08:08,183
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what matters.

108
00:08:08,643 --> 00:08:14,026
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you measure your practice not by how much you can go without food and how harsh you live and so on.

109
00:08:14,507 --> 00:08:16,408
[SPEAKER_01]: You measure it how...

110
00:08:17,732 --> 00:08:20,253
[SPEAKER_01]: How much more you're free from that delight.

111
00:08:21,154 --> 00:08:27,597
[SPEAKER_01]: How clearer you understood the nature of delight and how more apparent perception of danger in that delight is.

112
00:08:28,137 --> 00:08:31,899
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you would explain that to this man, he's like, oh, yeah, yeah, that is what's worthy of respect.

113
00:08:32,680 --> 00:08:36,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, you can easily find things to like or dislike on the on the base of a cover.

114
00:08:38,157 --> 00:08:47,779
[SPEAKER_01]: And sometimes, unfortunately, people would dismiss the message of a person, the teaching, because they don't approve of the cover, or it's not the right color, and so on.

115
00:08:48,359 --> 00:08:55,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, this man wasn't that shallow, but he was still equating practice with certain just, uh, austere kind of protocols.

116
00:08:56,260 --> 00:08:57,500
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says that's why he's a spiritualist.

117
00:08:57,560 --> 00:08:58,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Not at all.

118
00:08:58,500 --> 00:09:00,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, and then the other one was like, sometimes he wears silk robes.

119
00:09:02,161 --> 00:09:07,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, the silk robes made by boiling bugs to death, so they produce the silk.

120
00:09:08,154 --> 00:09:11,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, the Buddha did not ask for it, but somebody gave him the silk robes and he accepted it.

121
00:09:12,255 --> 00:09:16,637
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, from the ascetic point of view, that is absolutely outrageous.

122
00:09:17,537 --> 00:09:22,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just that he wears these comfortable silk robes, but also silk was made by killing animals.

123
00:09:22,738 --> 00:09:26,160
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and the Buddha did not say it's an offense, you mustn't wear it.

124
00:09:26,420 --> 00:09:30,161
[SPEAKER_01]: He said you mustn't ask for it to be killed, but if somebody does it and give it to you, it's fine.

125
00:09:30,681 --> 00:09:37,103
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he said he has monks that wear, like, absolute, the most roughest, basically barbed wire type of hemp robes.

126
00:09:38,593 --> 00:09:42,385
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that's the level of what should be respected, they will completely not respect it.

127
00:09:44,587 --> 00:09:50,791
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's basically knowing the hardwood, as the Buddha would say, from the external appearance of the wood, knowing what's the essence.

128
00:09:51,392 --> 00:09:51,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

129
00:09:52,312 --> 00:09:53,133
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the essence.

130
00:09:53,833 --> 00:10:00,078
[SPEAKER_01]: When it comes to any sensual restraint, you do it first, of course, out of faith, and out of that, kind of creating a buffer zone.

131
00:10:00,658 --> 00:10:04,000
[SPEAKER_01]: But, in itself, things you're restraining against were never the problem.

132
00:10:04,020 --> 00:10:05,121
[SPEAKER_01]: They're a secondary problem.

133
00:10:05,521 --> 00:10:06,642
[SPEAKER_01]: They're a symptom of your problem.

134
00:10:07,102 --> 00:10:08,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Symptom of your delight.

135
00:10:09,064 --> 00:10:12,026
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the thing you are directly responsible for.

136
00:10:12,626 --> 00:10:18,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Even when you have a sensual thought appearing in your mind, you did not create it in itself.

137
00:10:18,391 --> 00:10:21,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you can choose to entertain it and prolong it and revolve around it.

138
00:10:22,274 --> 00:10:24,396
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a new... and all that comes out of delight.

139
00:10:25,076 --> 00:10:30,441
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Buddha would say among dedicated to mindfulness and practice.

140
00:10:30,481 --> 00:10:35,004
[SPEAKER_01]: When a sensual thought arises, he doesn't delight in it.

141
00:10:35,024 --> 00:10:35,925
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't welcome it.

142
00:10:35,985 --> 00:10:37,006
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't entertain it.

143
00:10:38,267 --> 00:10:40,209
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's the same way we talked about before.

144
00:10:40,229 --> 00:10:41,309
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody comes at your door,

145
00:10:42,393 --> 00:10:48,136
[SPEAKER_01]: And once you get in, obviously, and now you feel pressured and you open the door, you either go and chase them or let them in and so on.

146
00:10:48,456 --> 00:10:52,599
[SPEAKER_01]: No, the point is to recognize, no, they can't get in unless I let them in.

147
00:10:53,079 --> 00:10:56,561
[SPEAKER_01]: But if I go out, well, that's another way of engaging with it, so they win.

148
00:10:57,061 --> 00:10:59,583
[SPEAKER_01]: If I let them in, that's another way of engaging, so they win again.

149
00:11:00,663 --> 00:11:07,487
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can I endure the pressure at the doorstep without letting it in, without basically losing my control and going out and shouting at them or something?

150
00:11:08,642 --> 00:11:11,384
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's how... and that's how they will leave.

151
00:11:12,004 --> 00:11:13,245
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you get rid of it.

152
00:11:14,446 --> 00:11:17,668
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't get rid of it by feeding it, by engaging with it, by acknowledging it.

153
00:11:18,129 --> 00:11:19,890
[SPEAKER_01]: You get rid of it by not acting out of it.

154
00:11:20,290 --> 00:11:23,232
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will not act out of it if you don't delight in it.

155
00:11:24,393 --> 00:11:28,336
[SPEAKER_01]: So delight is a necessary basis for acting towards sensuality.

156
00:11:28,556 --> 00:11:31,478
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot possibly act towards sensuality unless you're delighting in it.

157
00:11:32,368 --> 00:11:39,053
[SPEAKER_01]: And you cannot possibly delight in it, unless you lost or never had the sight of danger in that what you're delighting in.

158
00:11:40,354 --> 00:11:41,475
[SPEAKER_01]: So, cultivate non-delight.

159
00:11:41,495 --> 00:11:44,158
[SPEAKER_01]: You will literally experience dispassion towards the entire world.

160
00:11:44,218 --> 00:11:44,798
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

161
00:11:45,439 --> 00:11:52,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Up, down, left, right, past, future, beautiful, ugly, godly, humanly, whatever.

162
00:11:53,525 --> 00:11:55,727
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no delight in anything that appears for you.

163
00:11:59,057 --> 00:12:00,578
[SPEAKER_00]: And thus you have peaceful, yeah.

164
00:12:00,998 --> 00:12:01,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolute.

165
00:12:03,119 --> 00:12:03,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolute peace.

166
00:12:15,066 --> 00:12:16,687
[SPEAKER_00]: And perception number five.

167
00:12:17,788 --> 00:12:26,733
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, when a bhikkhu becomes accustomed to the perception of anicca, their mind draws back from acquisitions, honors and fame.

168
00:12:27,960 --> 00:12:32,342
[SPEAKER_00]: And either equanimity gets established, or the other side gets established.

169
00:12:33,963 --> 00:12:49,052
[SPEAKER_01]: So... Yeah, the other side, the counterpart of acquisition, ownership, faith, is a true understanding of that non-ownership, impossibility to own, as we often say when it comes to anicca.

170
00:12:50,964 --> 00:12:53,064
[SPEAKER_01]: See, if things are... Anicca...

171
00:12:53,144 --> 00:12:58,165
[SPEAKER_01]: I've said it before, I won't get into it again, but just to kind of mention again.

172
00:12:59,586 --> 00:13:01,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Anicca is not the impermanence that you can observe.

173
00:13:02,046 --> 00:13:04,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, look, I destroy this, thus it's impermanent.

174
00:13:05,347 --> 00:13:09,127
[SPEAKER_01]: The destruction of a thing is another thing that has arisen for you.

175
00:13:10,948 --> 00:13:12,468
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's only a relative destruction.

176
00:13:12,768 --> 00:13:14,448
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not destroying your experience as a whole.

177
00:13:14,568 --> 00:13:18,029
[SPEAKER_01]: You're experiencing this is present and now it's all chopped up.

178
00:13:19,113 --> 00:13:20,453
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean that this is impermanent.

179
00:13:20,553 --> 00:13:21,634
[SPEAKER_01]: It just means it has changed.

180
00:13:23,634 --> 00:13:24,915
[SPEAKER_01]: And you say, ah, so, change.

181
00:13:26,375 --> 00:13:27,575
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anicca is change.

182
00:13:29,916 --> 00:13:33,457
[SPEAKER_01]: But saying everything is changing, it's not personal enough.

183
00:13:34,397 --> 00:13:36,458
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to see that, yeah, everything is changing.

184
00:13:36,918 --> 00:13:44,240
[SPEAKER_01]: But also, me, I am, my experience, the whole, my ownership, my body, my senses, my feelings, my life.

185
00:13:44,820 --> 00:13:44,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh.

186
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:47,961
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that is also subject to change.

187
00:13:49,693 --> 00:13:50,274
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I'm saying.

188
00:13:50,454 --> 00:13:55,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Anicca is not about seeing the impermanence, because you can't see the impermanence of your life unless you're dead.

189
00:13:56,444 --> 00:13:57,405
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you can't see it.

190
00:13:57,986 --> 00:14:02,674
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you wait for death to show you that your life is subject to change, well, it's too late.

191
00:14:03,770 --> 00:14:04,470
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the whole point.

192
00:14:04,830 --> 00:14:11,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Saying that something is subject to change, means the possibility of change is inherent in it, without that thing changing.

193
00:14:12,613 --> 00:14:21,276
[SPEAKER_01]: So understanding that things are subject to change, subject to suffering, means understanding that before change, change will have to be inevitable.

194
00:14:21,536 --> 00:14:22,977
[SPEAKER_01]: So you don't wait for it to change.

195
00:14:23,017 --> 00:14:31,320
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to understand that while it's not changing, while your life is there, you want to understand that it's overall, fundamentally, going to change.

196
00:14:33,035 --> 00:14:40,121
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the nature of change is that nature of non-mastery, non-ownership, impossibility to control.

197
00:14:41,482 --> 00:14:45,065
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, you can control things within their arising, while they're present.

198
00:14:45,085 --> 00:14:46,566
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have a say in your life now.

199
00:14:47,046 --> 00:14:53,451
[SPEAKER_01]: But, fundamentally, you will not have a say when that life decides to change, as in you die.

200
00:14:53,471 --> 00:14:57,314
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like seeing the fundamental basis of your life.

201
00:14:57,374 --> 00:14:57,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Ownership.

202
00:14:58,255 --> 00:14:58,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

203
00:14:58,895 --> 00:14:59,096
[SPEAKER_01]: Your life.

204
00:14:59,116 --> 00:15:00,417
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever you're building up, yeah?

205
00:15:00,617 --> 00:15:00,817
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

206
00:15:07,452 --> 00:15:12,293
[SPEAKER_00]: Honours escalates into fame, escalates into that whole kind of... Extension of yourself.

207
00:15:12,374 --> 00:15:13,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

208
00:15:13,094 --> 00:15:13,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Extension of yourself.

209
00:15:13,894 --> 00:15:19,456
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the Buddha, when he was talking about those different layers of conceit that a monk who practices would remove.

210
00:15:20,216 --> 00:15:23,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, first, it would be the coarse sensuality and anger and...

211
00:15:23,577 --> 00:15:30,999
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, later on in the practice, he would be removing the thoughts of basically his nationality.

212
00:15:31,019 --> 00:15:35,941
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, on that general level of what you are, where you were, of a greater scale, more subtler.

213
00:15:37,281 --> 00:15:41,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, of his clan, of his family, of his country, or whatever.

214
00:15:42,006 --> 00:15:52,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you were not necessarily the great national patriot, or something, in your patriginal life, or whatever, you nevertheless, you still identified with the culture, the environment you lived through.

215
00:15:53,317 --> 00:15:55,619
[SPEAKER_01]: And those are subtler acquisitions.

216
00:15:55,679 --> 00:15:58,441
[SPEAKER_01]: But yes, acquisitions, honors, and fame.

217
00:15:58,502 --> 00:16:01,805
[SPEAKER_01]: So, fame is inherently accepted and liked by people.

218
00:16:03,204 --> 00:16:05,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Lack of fame is inherently feared by people.

219
00:16:05,265 --> 00:16:07,547
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why people irrationally feel fear of blame.

220
00:16:08,247 --> 00:16:14,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if there is no rational basis for a criticism, just the fact that this criticism met you, you're already defending against it and don't want it.

221
00:16:16,012 --> 00:16:21,135
[SPEAKER_01]: It takes a developed mind to not do that, to see through it, though the world warily wins.

222
00:16:21,875 --> 00:16:25,557
[SPEAKER_01]: And all of that comes from simple appropriation of the very things that are in front of you.

223
00:16:26,598 --> 00:16:31,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Your very experience, your very five aggregates, the feelings, the intentions, you already acquire them as yours.

224
00:16:33,668 --> 00:16:37,270
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you contemplate that not this is impermanent, that's impermanent.

225
00:16:37,630 --> 00:16:52,599
[SPEAKER_01]: My experience as a whole, in the utmost sort of general background of I am sitting here talking to you, while the dog is messing around, we are recording, all of that fundamentally is because my body with the senses is here operating and I cannot do anything about that.

226
00:16:52,999 --> 00:16:54,260
[SPEAKER_01]: And that subject to change.

227
00:16:55,140 --> 00:16:58,182
[SPEAKER_01]: As we said in other talks, if your heart would just stop,

228
00:16:59,058 --> 00:17:04,022
[SPEAKER_01]: None of this, my control within it, would be also taken.

229
00:17:04,582 --> 00:17:10,727
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I can control, I can move, I can say yes, I can say no, I can look this way, but all of that is within the non-control.

230
00:17:11,730 --> 00:17:14,512
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is within the basis that I have no access to.

231
00:17:14,712 --> 00:17:16,853
[SPEAKER_01]: I am not privy to the basis of my aggregates.

232
00:17:17,433 --> 00:17:23,337
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when they change, my entire domain, my entire world, life of control, will be swept away with it.

233
00:17:23,797 --> 00:17:24,878
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can recognize that.

234
00:17:25,398 --> 00:17:32,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Through, like, your experience right now, that is in your control, you can recognize that, fundamentally, this is all subjected to change.

235
00:17:32,942 --> 00:17:33,282
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

236
00:17:33,383 --> 00:17:34,643
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to wait for it to change.

237
00:17:34,663 --> 00:17:37,445
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to make it change to prove yourself it's subject to change.

238
00:17:38,353 --> 00:17:44,956
[SPEAKER_01]: You just need to stop acting out of it unwholesomely and discern that, whether you want it or not, the subject of change is implicit.

239
00:17:45,637 --> 00:17:47,037
[SPEAKER_01]: It defines the thing, pretty much.

240
00:17:47,338 --> 00:17:49,639
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence the understanding of anicca.

241
00:17:51,019 --> 00:18:01,085
[SPEAKER_01]: And then there is... then, even if, oh, this is mine, emotionally, there is no acquiring of that, because emotionally, you're already approved... well, you approved your emotions by seeing that they're subject to change.

242
00:18:02,740 --> 00:18:05,841
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you then possibly be emotional on account of something secondary to it?

243
00:18:06,241 --> 00:18:08,002
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't take things personally anymore.

244
00:18:08,122 --> 00:18:08,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

245
00:18:09,622 --> 00:18:11,843
[SPEAKER_00]: But because you have, you won't go further.

246
00:18:12,123 --> 00:18:12,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

247
00:18:12,383 --> 00:18:16,004
[SPEAKER_00]: That perception of Nietzsche won't take you further into taking things personally.

248
00:18:16,024 --> 00:18:16,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

249
00:18:16,584 --> 00:18:18,505
[SPEAKER_01]: And it will start undoing.

250
00:18:18,545 --> 00:18:25,307
[SPEAKER_01]: If you cultivate that perception for Nietzsche, sustain it, maintain it in its being, bhavana, literally,

251
00:18:26,168 --> 00:18:38,763
[SPEAKER_01]: It would result in completely fading away of all the acquisitions, which would result in fading away of all the, like, honours, depending on honours, notions of conceit, the greatness, the clan, the belonging, the sense of self in the greatest sense.

252
00:18:39,544 --> 00:18:42,207
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you will be completely free of the slightest traces of conceit.

253
00:18:44,768 --> 00:18:49,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Like you're saying the other day, and that's, I think, that's what going forth is.

254
00:18:49,970 --> 00:18:50,930
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the going forth.

255
00:18:51,030 --> 00:18:54,811
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the actually establishing of anicca.

256
00:18:54,831 --> 00:18:54,972
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

257
00:18:55,612 --> 00:19:04,795
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the real... Well, it's like basically establishing of that my life is subject to anicca in a more kind of acute manner.

258
00:19:05,515 --> 00:19:16,846
[SPEAKER_01]: See, when you live in your home, with your family, with your routine, with your village, with your province, in your country, all of these are like layers and layers and layers of acquisition.

259
00:19:17,366 --> 00:19:18,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Familiarity.

260
00:19:18,207 --> 00:19:19,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Familiarity, exactly, exactly.

261
00:19:19,969 --> 00:19:21,270
[SPEAKER_01]: And why is familiarity...

262
00:19:23,271 --> 00:19:23,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Value.

263
00:19:24,132 --> 00:19:25,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it doesn't change.

264
00:19:26,053 --> 00:19:27,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Not as often, at least.

265
00:19:27,273 --> 00:19:28,554
[SPEAKER_01]: No, my family, his traditions...

266
00:19:28,574 --> 00:19:29,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly, exactly.

267
00:19:30,215 --> 00:19:32,297
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why do you then... Oh, my family changed.

268
00:19:32,317 --> 00:19:34,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, but at least we are still the clan.

269
00:19:34,158 --> 00:19:35,258
[SPEAKER_01]: We still have the family name.

270
00:19:36,239 --> 00:19:37,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, but at least we're this way.

271
00:19:37,340 --> 00:19:39,261
[SPEAKER_01]: At least we're in this country and so on.

272
00:19:39,281 --> 00:19:39,761
[SPEAKER_01]: We, like...

273
00:19:39,981 --> 00:19:41,442
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have this greater and greater identity.

274
00:19:41,482 --> 00:19:41,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

275
00:19:42,102 --> 00:19:43,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it doesn't change as much as the other one.

276
00:19:44,304 --> 00:19:45,505
[SPEAKER_01]: And why do you don't like change?

277
00:19:45,525 --> 00:19:49,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, because it reminds you of the very, in the very core of your being, you are not your own.

278
00:19:50,636 --> 00:19:54,103
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's something that people throughout their entire existence tries to outrun.

279
00:19:54,684 --> 00:19:56,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to cover up and keep the lid on.

280
00:19:56,869 --> 00:19:57,790
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's the norm.

281
00:19:59,661 --> 00:20:02,023
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, if someone wants to go forth...

282
00:20:02,043 --> 00:20:02,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

283
00:20:02,523 --> 00:20:06,066
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to remove these false safety zones that you created.

284
00:20:06,466 --> 00:20:08,227
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not understand it immediately.

285
00:20:08,287 --> 00:20:09,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, some did.

286
00:20:09,328 --> 00:20:11,289
[SPEAKER_01]: As soon as they went forth, they became arahants.

287
00:20:11,329 --> 00:20:12,730
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the meaning of going forth.

288
00:20:12,750 --> 00:20:13,251
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in a way...

289
00:20:13,271 --> 00:20:16,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Meaning is to start emulating what you need to abandon before it's too late.

290
00:20:17,694 --> 00:20:18,214
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

291
00:20:18,234 --> 00:20:25,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, today, often, you have people just go into their... into the monastery, but it's not really that they left the home.

292
00:20:26,340 --> 00:20:29,142
[SPEAKER_01]: They just replaced one more, like a...

293
00:20:30,045 --> 00:20:36,448
[SPEAKER_01]: A smaller setup of a family and familiarity, with a bigger setup of a bigger family and familiarity, i.e.

294
00:20:36,528 --> 00:20:37,088
[SPEAKER_01]: tradition.

295
00:20:37,929 --> 00:20:46,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is the way, and this is the way, and everybody must... And there's no thinking for oneself, because if you start thinking for yourself, you realize, oh well, all of this is secondary.

296
00:20:46,793 --> 00:20:53,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Tradition, family name, I'm such-and-such monk, this is such-and-such my upajaya, this is what... All of that is secondary.

297
00:20:54,056 --> 00:20:57,618
[SPEAKER_01]: What is first is that you choose to be there for the reasons of safety,

298
00:20:58,555 --> 00:21:00,196
[SPEAKER_01]: But, like, why do I need safety?

299
00:21:01,337 --> 00:21:03,339
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you protecting?

300
00:21:03,359 --> 00:21:12,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you're protecting sense of ownership, you're protecting yourself from anxiety, you're protecting your feelings, you're protecting from uncertainty, because you might overwhelm your mind.

301
00:21:12,687 --> 00:21:22,575
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize that today you can have somebody gone forth for decades and still basically a layman, mentally.

302
00:21:22,595 --> 00:21:23,196
[SPEAKER_01]: You just kind of

303
00:21:24,453 --> 00:21:27,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Extended the setup of a home into a bigger area.

304
00:21:27,858 --> 00:21:35,809
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, it's not as... it's not as... as... narrowed, as like a little familiarity of my little nook in my house.

305
00:21:36,817 --> 00:21:42,682
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you do kind of expand a little bit, but overall, you haven't really... you haven't really abandoned everything.

306
00:21:42,762 --> 00:21:48,127
[SPEAKER_01]: All of these buffer zones that your own conceit needs to protect its own ownership and sense of self.

307
00:21:48,347 --> 00:21:52,491
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... if you're... you're born into the Buddhist tradition.

308
00:21:52,551 --> 00:21:53,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

309
00:21:53,972 --> 00:21:54,333
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm talking...

310
00:21:54,353 --> 00:21:54,673
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm talking...

311
00:21:54,693 --> 00:21:55,754
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not even talking about that.

312
00:21:55,834 --> 00:22:02,140
[SPEAKER_01]: But, no, that's even more... that's more like... well, that's just... you become part of... You never leave the society by being a priest.

313
00:22:03,702 --> 00:22:08,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're just part of the the social structure, what priests do, for whom... That's your career.

314
00:22:08,466 --> 00:22:09,707
[SPEAKER_00]: You've never left home, still.

315
00:22:09,727 --> 00:22:09,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

316
00:22:09,967 --> 00:22:10,808
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not talking about that.

317
00:22:10,828 --> 00:22:14,771
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm talking for people who do want to practice and kind of go forth, but then you realize...

318
00:22:15,731 --> 00:22:18,594
[SPEAKER_01]: But now you just replaced one familiarity with another familiarity.

319
00:22:19,514 --> 00:22:21,656
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you're afraid to leave this second familiarity.

320
00:22:22,076 --> 00:22:24,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Even to the point of... You remember that... You've seen it many times.

321
00:22:24,238 --> 00:22:25,619
[SPEAKER_01]: People are afraid to change the monastery.

322
00:22:25,959 --> 00:22:27,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Just for a period of time.

323
00:22:27,961 --> 00:22:32,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Or even... Or let alone change the monastery that's in a different tradition.

324
00:22:33,538 --> 00:22:35,599
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's just like, whoa, whoa, whoa.

325
00:22:35,619 --> 00:22:36,820
[SPEAKER_01]: But wait, you are homeless.

326
00:22:38,240 --> 00:22:40,101
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why would this be such a big deal?

327
00:22:40,521 --> 00:22:45,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Or like certain places, well, I can't go there because, you know, the routine is too different there.

328
00:22:45,464 --> 00:22:46,244
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no routine.

329
00:22:46,284 --> 00:22:47,825
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the worst fear people would have.

330
00:22:48,465 --> 00:22:50,006
[SPEAKER_01]: When they come, what's your... there is no routine.

331
00:22:50,106 --> 00:22:50,826
[SPEAKER_01]: You're on your own.

332
00:22:51,747 --> 00:22:57,189
[SPEAKER_01]: So, nobody... there's no structure, no traditional authority demanding from you what to do throughout the day.

333
00:22:58,069 --> 00:22:59,510
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is the most frightening thing.

334
00:23:00,691 --> 00:23:00,751
[SPEAKER_01]: If

335
00:23:01,546 --> 00:23:05,590
[SPEAKER_01]: You're basically not honest with yourself fundamentally and realize that that's where the practice is.

336
00:23:05,950 --> 00:23:07,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's where the going forth is.

337
00:23:07,311 --> 00:23:10,153
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the going forth... literally, there is... and that's what they said.

338
00:23:10,214 --> 00:23:17,300
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... once you do it rightly in the suttas, nothing can match that level of freedom, like a... like a mountain deer.

339
00:23:18,448 --> 00:23:22,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, anything lower than that would be feeling like a trap in prison.

340
00:23:23,050 --> 00:23:23,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, dusty.

341
00:23:23,951 --> 00:23:25,071
[SPEAKER_00]: The home life is dusty.

342
00:23:25,231 --> 00:23:27,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the home life is crowded and dusty.

343
00:23:27,632 --> 00:23:28,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Many people frequent it.

344
00:23:29,353 --> 00:23:30,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Constant hustle and bustle.

345
00:23:31,394 --> 00:23:36,857
[SPEAKER_00]: But, now, even the monastic life is crowded and dusty.

346
00:23:36,877 --> 00:23:38,258
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it can be, yeah.

347
00:23:38,438 --> 00:23:47,102
[SPEAKER_01]: You just go from one... And it happened even in the suttas, when the Buddha reprimanded Ananda, when they were... And they weren't even, like, you know, they were all probably

348
00:23:47,982 --> 00:23:50,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Āryas, at least, or maybe even some of them were arahants.

349
00:23:50,585 --> 00:23:51,005
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

350
00:23:51,025 --> 00:23:54,649
[SPEAKER_01]: When the Buddha came, he was like, what's this noise and racket here?

351
00:23:57,391 --> 00:24:03,217
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, oh, we're sewing robes and organizing cloth, all for a very good purpose.

352
00:24:03,897 --> 00:24:08,882
[SPEAKER_01]: And he told them off for being in a crowd of monks, not like outside the lay people or something.

353
00:24:09,843 --> 00:24:12,526
[SPEAKER_01]: So you could say like, okay, so if that's not acceptable,

354
00:24:14,068 --> 00:24:22,433
[SPEAKER_01]: How much more that's true for, like, just, you know, being fully engaged physically in the crowd, in society, in people, and so on.

355
00:24:23,013 --> 00:24:30,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you need to do something, give some teaching one way or the other, but to what extent that compromises the lifestyle of solitude.

356
00:24:30,518 --> 00:24:33,400
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're a monk that's gone from home, from family life,

357
00:24:34,060 --> 00:24:35,681
[SPEAKER_00]: To another temple.

358
00:24:36,062 --> 00:24:36,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Temple life.

359
00:24:36,762 --> 00:24:39,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's even more crowded than your home beforehand.

360
00:24:39,984 --> 00:24:44,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, I mean, that's quite common, but then you have to go forth.

361
00:24:44,327 --> 00:24:46,088
[SPEAKER_00]: You still have to go forth.

362
00:24:46,349 --> 00:24:46,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

363
00:24:47,589 --> 00:24:55,435
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that monk who's stuck in that familiar tradition of Buddhist monasticism needs to actually go forth from that.

364
00:24:55,535 --> 00:24:56,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Needs to go forth, yeah.

365
00:24:57,356 --> 00:24:59,678
[SPEAKER_00]: Can he stop being a monk?

366
00:25:00,627 --> 00:25:03,209
[SPEAKER_01]: But still, without... Yeah, without disrobing.

367
00:25:03,409 --> 00:25:04,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Without disrobing.

368
00:25:04,069 --> 00:25:04,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

369
00:25:04,930 --> 00:25:08,011
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's that's the thing, like... Can you still practice?

370
00:25:08,031 --> 00:25:20,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Your practice should be defined by, like, as I said, it starts, of course, by sense restraint, virtue, learning the rules, but if you never upgrade it from that level, you are still defined by these random observances.

371
00:25:20,899 --> 00:25:26,182
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, literally, virtue and my duty is the very first fetter that's sought upon abandons.

372
00:25:27,466 --> 00:25:28,447
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what it is.

373
00:25:28,887 --> 00:25:36,030
[SPEAKER_01]: He leaves it without breaking it, but not holding it anymore, not holding it as an acquisition, so to speak.

374
00:25:38,532 --> 00:25:40,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you realize, ah, it's about the non-delight.

375
00:25:41,153 --> 00:25:48,176
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion, that you get on account of sense restraint, if you made the right effort.

376
00:25:49,017 --> 00:25:52,559
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you can literally be a monk anywhere, in any circumstance.

377
00:25:53,830 --> 00:25:54,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not about what you do.

378
00:25:55,251 --> 00:25:57,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Not even, like, what you wear, so to speak.

379
00:25:58,393 --> 00:26:00,274
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, like, an external statement for others.

380
00:26:01,895 --> 00:26:03,836
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about what you're incapable of thinking.

381
00:26:04,537 --> 00:26:06,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Why you're incapable of pursuing internally.

382
00:26:06,858 --> 00:26:08,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything based on greed, aversion, delusion.

383
00:26:12,982 --> 00:26:14,743
[SPEAKER_01]: So, non-delight brings dispassion.

384
00:26:15,324 --> 00:26:15,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Change?

385
00:26:17,245 --> 00:26:20,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing the you-yourself in its core of your very being,

386
00:26:21,484 --> 00:26:23,066
[SPEAKER_01]: are subject to change.

387
00:26:23,967 --> 00:26:25,949
[SPEAKER_01]: That's enough to uproot your sense of ownership.

388
00:26:27,732 --> 00:26:30,194
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, in what you see, there is just what you see.

389
00:26:30,215 --> 00:26:31,596
[SPEAKER_01]: In what you hear, there's just what you hear.

390
00:26:31,977 --> 00:26:34,460
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like more, less, want, don't want.

391
00:26:35,687 --> 00:26:39,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is agreeable, this is disagreeable, this is comfortable, this is uncomfortable.

392
00:26:39,910 --> 00:26:43,813
[SPEAKER_01]: None of it is besetting or overwhelming my mind.

393
00:26:44,353 --> 00:26:51,959
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's... this is... your mind draws back from fame or reputation, really, from... Yeah, exactly.

394
00:26:51,979 --> 00:26:52,720
[SPEAKER_00]: ...disrepute.

395
00:26:52,880 --> 00:26:59,765
[SPEAKER_00]: So, oh, those people, my family, might think that I'm this complete... Yeah, what other people will think about you.

396
00:26:59,905 --> 00:27:00,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

397
00:27:00,585 --> 00:27:02,727
[SPEAKER_01]: That will... that ceases to be your concern.

398
00:27:04,687 --> 00:27:08,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he's been justly or unjustly criticized, doesn't matter.

399
00:27:08,810 --> 00:27:12,994
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not lose perspective on it on account of that irrational fear of blame.

400
00:27:14,795 --> 00:27:19,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, and the pressure to make sure that they have the right view of you.

401
00:27:19,939 --> 00:27:21,501
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, that's gone as well.

402
00:27:21,521 --> 00:27:22,782
[SPEAKER_01]: That's rooted in your conceit.

403
00:27:24,163 --> 00:27:27,405
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if people... Again, you're trying to control that which is uncontrollable.

404
00:27:27,766 --> 00:27:29,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Other people's opinions, literally.

405
00:27:30,274 --> 00:27:32,335
[SPEAKER_01]: But, okay, many people can understand that.

406
00:27:32,815 --> 00:27:42,761
[SPEAKER_01]: But one thing is to understand that on a rational level, and another thing is to let that rational level settle in, so you're emotionally unperturbed, in regard to blame and criticism.

407
00:27:43,301 --> 00:27:45,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Even emotionally, it cannot be touched.

408
00:27:45,583 --> 00:27:47,784
[SPEAKER_01]: Let alone mentally revolve around it.

409
00:27:49,225 --> 00:27:51,106
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you will be unable to lose perspective.

410
00:27:51,126 --> 00:27:57,930
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody criticizes you, you would be absolutely clear about where they're coming from, and you could see, actually, they have a point.

411
00:27:58,783 --> 00:28:00,264
[SPEAKER_00]: I was completely wrong in that regard.

412
00:28:00,365 --> 00:28:01,906
[SPEAKER_00]: And you wouldn't have a problem admitting it either.

413
00:28:03,187 --> 00:28:08,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not irrationally defending your conceit, your gratuitous ownership.

414
00:28:09,112 --> 00:28:12,394
[SPEAKER_01]: And equally, if you're criticizing, you realize that's absolutely ridiculous.

415
00:28:12,454 --> 00:28:13,215
[SPEAKER_01]: That's even not true.

416
00:28:13,495 --> 00:28:15,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely misses the point.

417
00:28:15,157 --> 00:28:16,698
[SPEAKER_01]: But you wouldn't feel pressure to...

418
00:28:17,238 --> 00:28:22,002
[SPEAKER_01]: I must correct and rectify the world and make sure that everybody knows the truth.

419
00:28:22,122 --> 00:28:23,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Defend your honor.

420
00:28:23,964 --> 00:28:24,644
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

421
00:28:24,664 --> 00:28:25,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Defend your honor.

422
00:28:25,265 --> 00:28:25,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Defend your name.

423
00:28:29,610 --> 00:28:34,433
[SPEAKER_01]: So, reputation, like, again, it's like sense-restraint.

424
00:28:35,995 --> 00:28:44,801
[SPEAKER_01]: Initially, you want to practice sense-restraint, and you want to keep the vinaya rules, and you don't want to break that reputation.

425
00:28:46,942 --> 00:28:48,163
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you don't want to break the virtue.

426
00:28:49,284 --> 00:28:54,608
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you never upgrade from that level, you're still remaining dependent on circumstances.

427
00:28:55,817 --> 00:29:13,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, if circumstances then criticize you, even if you haven't actually did anything to break your reputation, just somebody doesn't like you, or they disapprove of you in any other way, and then they just find things to blame you for, you'll be affected by it, because you stayed on that level where these things, where these things are subjected to that.

428
00:29:14,365 --> 00:29:17,746
[SPEAKER_01]: You're depending on the circumstances not criticizing you, not blaming you.

429
00:29:20,066 --> 00:29:21,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, your your equanimity is based...

430
00:29:21,667 --> 00:29:22,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Your equanimity is dependent on that.

431
00:29:22,947 --> 00:29:24,468
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not equanimity.

432
00:29:24,488 --> 00:29:26,168
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a very limited equanimity.

433
00:29:26,188 --> 00:29:27,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Shaky ground, basically.

434
00:29:27,429 --> 00:29:28,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Very shaky ground, yeah.

435
00:29:28,389 --> 00:29:34,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, literally, so my my well-being depends on the opinion of others.

436
00:29:35,232 --> 00:29:36,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I can't even...

437
00:29:36,473 --> 00:29:38,813
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't have a say in how my own thoughts arise.

438
00:29:39,534 --> 00:29:41,595
[SPEAKER_01]: I only have a say in whether I delight in them or not.

439
00:29:43,075 --> 00:29:47,497
[SPEAKER_01]: And here I am, assuming I should have a say in how other people's thoughts arise and what they think of me.

440
00:29:48,149 --> 00:29:48,309
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

441
00:29:48,570 --> 00:29:53,175
[SPEAKER_01]: All I can do is not delight in it, not try to reject it or resist it irrationally.

442
00:29:53,556 --> 00:29:54,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Same what you do to yourself.

443
00:29:54,677 --> 00:29:57,521
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why that he knows it internally, he knows it externally.

444
00:29:57,641 --> 00:30:02,567
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same thing, same attitude in regard to both, or internally and externally.

445
00:30:10,880 --> 00:30:15,186
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, then you're kind of bulletproof for whatever happens externally.

446
00:30:15,426 --> 00:30:19,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you basically left that shore where these things are happening.

447
00:30:19,812 --> 00:30:20,793
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not there anymore.

448
00:30:21,334 --> 00:30:22,295
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing for you.

449
00:30:22,876 --> 00:30:23,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing, nothing.

450
00:30:23,897 --> 00:30:25,539
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible for anything to land on you.

451
00:30:26,564 --> 00:30:27,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're simply not there.

452
00:30:27,705 --> 00:30:29,745
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to psychologize, or... No.

453
00:30:29,986 --> 00:30:32,086
[SPEAKER_00]: ...the reasons that other people are saying these things to you.

454
00:30:32,106 --> 00:30:33,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and that's another thing.

455
00:30:33,167 --> 00:30:46,653
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, like when people sometimes say that, like, I don't know, somebody offends you, and you're really angry, and you can't... you still bear the grudge, and then you find out that that person was abused as a child, and now you're not angry with them.

456
00:30:47,173 --> 00:30:48,354
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's just psychologizing.

457
00:30:48,374 --> 00:30:49,594
[SPEAKER_01]: You never uprooted the anger.

458
00:30:50,214 --> 00:30:55,977
[SPEAKER_01]: You just basically updated the circumstances, and now this is acceptable for you to not be angry.

459
00:30:56,607 --> 00:31:04,676
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you don't want to be angry in the first place, regardless of person who offended you, was sort of abused and went through lots of suffering or didn't.

460
00:31:04,716 --> 00:31:06,638
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to be offended by full stop.

461
00:31:07,799 --> 00:31:10,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Because offense is on you, it's not on what's being said.

462
00:31:11,860 --> 00:31:19,945
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you need to justify the source of the offense as, ah, see, I forgive him because such and such, ah, you still blame what's being said for offense.

463
00:31:19,965 --> 00:31:22,647
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not taking responsibility for it on the emotional level.

464
00:31:23,007 --> 00:31:24,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, sooner or later, that will have to change.

465
00:31:25,649 --> 00:31:29,231
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sooner or later, you'll be back to suffer again on account of some other offense.

466
00:31:29,913 --> 00:31:35,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Because what stops you to find out that, actually, no, they were not abused as a child, and they were not a victim.

467
00:31:35,997 --> 00:31:37,638
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you can find out again, no, they really were.

468
00:31:38,199 --> 00:31:39,359
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you can find out that they weren't.

469
00:31:39,560 --> 00:31:52,729
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're fully thrown dependent on the worldly winds, praise, blame, fame, and you just, ah, left, right, trying to avoid one, failing to see that the other one will have to come because of it.

470
00:31:52,749 --> 00:31:57,072
[SPEAKER_01]: So, seeing that the problem is not in the world, in the movements of the world,

471
00:31:57,992 --> 00:32:01,255
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in your own delight, non-delight, resistance or craving against it.

472
00:32:02,116 --> 00:32:04,518
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop doing that, it doesn't matter.

473
00:32:04,838 --> 00:32:06,499
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you don't have to manage the world anymore.

474
00:32:06,860 --> 00:32:07,760
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the true freedom.

475
00:32:08,601 --> 00:32:10,343
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you're not burdened by action.

476
00:32:10,903 --> 00:32:13,465
[SPEAKER_01]: The things you have to do, otherwise you'll drown in your own suffering.

477
00:32:15,827 --> 00:32:20,471
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... the continuation of this Sutta, 7, in Gujarat 7, 49.

478
00:32:22,732 --> 00:32:23,993
[SPEAKER_00]: This is perception number six.

479
00:32:25,014 --> 00:32:44,248
[SPEAKER_00]: When a bhikkhu becomes accustomed to the perception of dukkha in anicca, an acute perception of fear or danger becomes settled in him towards indolence, laziness, slackness, heedlessness, lack of effort and unreflectiveness.

480
00:32:45,582 --> 00:32:50,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Just as one would have fear towards one's executioner with a drawn-out sword.

481
00:32:52,028 --> 00:32:53,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, time is short, actually.

482
00:32:53,529 --> 00:32:57,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so, yeah, anicca becomes established.

483
00:32:57,933 --> 00:33:06,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because that's why, like, regardless of what you do, what you think, you remain subject to change, which means at any time it can occur in an unfavorable manner.

484
00:33:07,861 --> 00:33:12,125
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once you recognize the weight of that, the gravity of that situation,

485
00:33:12,998 --> 00:33:14,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, that's uncomfortable.

486
00:33:14,539 --> 00:33:15,959
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the dukkha in that change.

487
00:33:18,100 --> 00:33:26,584
[SPEAKER_01]: The inherent unpleasantness in that not being your own, not being in your own control, not having a say over your own life, fundamentally.

488
00:33:26,624 --> 00:33:31,046
[SPEAKER_01]: It depends upon external random things, such as functioning organs, that you have no say in whatsoever.

489
00:33:31,746 --> 00:33:37,408
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you recognize that anicca correctly, you will be recognizing the discomfort of that situation.

490
00:33:38,009 --> 00:33:41,030
[SPEAKER_01]: And the clearer it is, the more of the sense of urgency will develop.

491
00:33:42,914 --> 00:33:49,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if I choose to ignore it, or rest, go to sleep, or whatever else, no, think about it, I remain subject to it.

492
00:33:49,697 --> 00:33:50,677
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can see...

493
00:33:50,697 --> 00:33:57,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, it's a dear... like, everything is at stake, at any given time.

494
00:33:58,540 --> 00:34:01,722
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can see, you know, am I being lazy?

495
00:34:01,982 --> 00:34:05,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I sitting back, relaxed, and enjoying things?

496
00:34:06,644 --> 00:34:09,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's the thing, and that's... you would also then understand what true laziness is.

497
00:34:09,965 --> 00:34:12,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Sitting back and relaxing is not necessarily laziness.

498
00:34:12,947 --> 00:34:17,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Mentally, as we discussed in that talk, delusion, delusion, that's what laziness is.

499
00:34:18,049 --> 00:34:23,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Mentally giving in to oblivion, turning away, distracting yourself, ignoring.

500
00:34:24,533 --> 00:34:27,776
[SPEAKER_01]: You can just spend the whole day laying on the bed, if you want.

501
00:34:28,256 --> 00:34:33,341
[SPEAKER_01]: But not for a second, should you allow your mind to forget about the perilous situation that it's in.

502
00:34:35,042 --> 00:34:37,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Undermined by change that's not in your control.

503
00:34:38,586 --> 00:34:42,169
[SPEAKER_01]: And you'll be practicing contemplation and meditation, regardless of your body posture.

504
00:34:43,557 --> 00:34:49,021
[SPEAKER_00]: But also, you can't entertain the idea that you've understood anicca, dukkha...

505
00:34:49,482 --> 00:34:50,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless you're feeling that urgency.

506
00:34:51,003 --> 00:34:52,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and there's no urgency.

507
00:34:52,444 --> 00:34:53,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

508
00:34:53,245 --> 00:34:54,145
[SPEAKER_01]: But that hasn't been the case.

509
00:34:54,165 --> 00:35:02,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you're either in arahant, because there's no urgency, you're fully free, so there's nothing to burgeon about, or you don't even understand what anicca is.

510
00:35:04,802 --> 00:35:05,683
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's a clear sign.

511
00:35:05,723 --> 00:35:07,904
[SPEAKER_00]: There can be a clear red flag for you.

512
00:35:07,944 --> 00:35:08,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

513
00:35:08,184 --> 00:35:13,208
[SPEAKER_01]: If you practice anicca correctly, that discomfort of dukkha will be apparent.

514
00:35:13,408 --> 00:35:15,789
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot practice anicca and not feel the dukkha.

515
00:35:19,492 --> 00:35:24,215
[SPEAKER_00]: But laziness can be a sign of that danger you're in, that you haven't understood.

516
00:35:24,455 --> 00:35:25,336
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

517
00:35:25,356 --> 00:35:29,699
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that you can still turn and engage in laziness, yeah.

518
00:35:29,959 --> 00:35:30,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what we said.

519
00:35:30,839 --> 00:35:31,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Even, like,

520
00:35:33,070 --> 00:35:39,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Satipaṭṭhāna, sakadāgāmī, anāgāmī, if you were to see them, he would be sort of trying to re-evoke the sense of urgency in them.

521
00:35:39,814 --> 00:35:40,434
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, why?

522
00:35:40,534 --> 00:35:42,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, because there is still work to be done.

523
00:35:43,236 --> 00:35:46,118
[SPEAKER_01]: And if he would see an arahant, he wouldn't try to invoke sense of urgency in him.

524
00:35:48,499 --> 00:35:48,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

525
00:35:48,779 --> 00:35:50,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is nothing to be done anymore.

526
00:35:52,022 --> 00:35:52,462
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's...

527
00:35:52,903 --> 00:35:54,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, to be done.

528
00:35:55,024 --> 00:35:55,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Work to be done.

529
00:35:56,325 --> 00:35:59,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like experiences that might happen to me if I'm lucky.

530
00:35:59,948 --> 00:36:03,992
[SPEAKER_01]: No, literally, you and your effort is the sole determination.

531
00:36:05,873 --> 00:36:11,017
[SPEAKER_01]: If you sustain the effort, and do it, and do it, and do it, the results will have to follow.

532
00:36:11,417 --> 00:36:15,801
[SPEAKER_01]: If there are no results, it's either because you're not doing enough effort, or your effort is misplaced.

533
00:36:16,582 --> 00:36:18,183
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you're doing wrong things.

534
00:36:18,303 --> 00:36:19,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Putting effort in wrong things.

535
00:36:21,178 --> 00:36:22,679
[SPEAKER_00]: You're hoping things will just work out.

536
00:36:22,759 --> 00:36:23,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

537
00:36:23,139 --> 00:36:23,959
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just doing this randomly.

538
00:36:23,979 --> 00:36:26,360
[SPEAKER_01]: But do you recognize what you're doing as a practice?

539
00:36:26,420 --> 00:36:26,900
[SPEAKER_01]: It's work.

540
00:36:27,280 --> 00:36:35,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you recognize that as pertaining to freedom from suffering, freedom from delight, inability to suffer amidst the worldly winds?

541
00:36:35,583 --> 00:36:37,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you recognize that for fact?

542
00:36:37,624 --> 00:36:39,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see a relationship between the two?

543
00:36:40,065 --> 00:36:43,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Or it's more like, yeah, yeah, my life's getting better, but I don't quite see the connection.

544
00:36:43,706 --> 00:36:45,787
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just doing this because, you know, it makes me feel good.

545
00:36:46,867 --> 00:36:48,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you don't really know what the work is.

546
00:36:48,748 --> 00:36:52,089
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you then do the work if you don't know what the actual work is?

547
00:36:52,770 --> 00:36:54,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Hey, can you build me something?

548
00:36:54,590 --> 00:36:55,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Build me a table.

549
00:36:55,991 --> 00:36:57,732
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have a clue how to build a table.

550
00:36:57,792 --> 00:36:58,992
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you possibly build a table?

551
00:36:59,232 --> 00:37:02,754
[SPEAKER_01]: First, you need to understand what the work of building a table is, to produce a table.

552
00:37:05,415 --> 00:37:07,536
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the work of non-delight.

553
00:37:08,376 --> 00:37:11,478
[SPEAKER_01]: The work of penetrating through and understanding the nature of things.

554
00:37:13,178 --> 00:37:14,719
[SPEAKER_01]: The work of knowing where the problem is.

555
00:37:15,923 --> 00:37:17,844
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is not in that somebody's in front of my door.

556
00:37:18,504 --> 00:37:20,705
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is that I can't help being affected by it.

557
00:37:20,825 --> 00:37:24,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Either open the door, try to chase them, or open the door, let them in.

558
00:37:24,586 --> 00:37:24,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way.

559
00:37:26,667 --> 00:37:28,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you just think, oh, I must chase them.

560
00:37:29,208 --> 00:37:33,089
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're making all this effort, sensory strain, sensory strain, completely wrong on a wrong basis.

561
00:37:33,889 --> 00:37:38,851
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't understand where the problem is.

562
00:37:38,871 --> 00:37:41,532
[SPEAKER_00]: But when you see that danger, you're surrounded by...

563
00:37:41,772 --> 00:37:44,213
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you already did a fair amount of work.

564
00:37:45,545 --> 00:37:55,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Because danger is not seen because the entire life, the entire existence is about, as I said, outrunning it and covering it up, keeping it at arm's length.

565
00:37:56,668 --> 00:38:11,513
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you manage to see the danger, either circumstances force it upon you, but then, again, many people experience the danger and the peril and fear for their lives and so on, but they don't get wisdom on account of it, because the moment the circumstances change, they're back into their old ways.

566
00:38:12,465 --> 00:38:16,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people, however, don't even need to experience the circumstances at all.

567
00:38:16,107 --> 00:38:18,508
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is recognize that they are subjected to all of this.

568
00:38:18,548 --> 00:38:22,691
[SPEAKER_01]: And that, in itself, is all the danger you need.

569
00:38:22,731 --> 00:38:23,471
[SPEAKER_00]: All the recognition.

570
00:38:23,571 --> 00:38:24,972
[SPEAKER_01]: All the recognition you need, yeah.

571
00:38:27,822 --> 00:38:37,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that recognition, as well, of being completely surrounded, or, you know, the executioner's sword is on your neck, you're tied up, at any point he's going to do it.

572
00:38:39,147 --> 00:38:39,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

573
00:38:39,567 --> 00:38:43,928
[SPEAKER_01]: That could freak people, you know, that's... Yeah, well, they free them out because, again, they don't know what the problem is.

574
00:38:44,729 --> 00:38:47,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you would think the problem is... you would think you can avoid the sword.

575
00:38:47,930 --> 00:38:48,330
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

576
00:38:48,991 --> 00:38:50,091
[SPEAKER_01]: He will cut your head off.

577
00:38:53,155 --> 00:39:03,901
[SPEAKER_01]: But people don't realize that, so they still act out of this notion that they're in control of their life, so they just have to be extra careful and don't make a wrong move, so the executioner will cut their head off.

578
00:39:04,001 --> 00:39:05,402
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's not about making a wrong move.

579
00:39:05,722 --> 00:39:12,687
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why people quote that sutta to justify this ridiculous sensation watching practice of moving, moving, sitting.

580
00:39:12,727 --> 00:39:15,008
[SPEAKER_01]: I must not let a second slip, because they'll chop my head off.

581
00:39:15,028 --> 00:39:15,148
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

582
00:39:15,388 --> 00:39:16,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Your head's being chopped anyway.

583
00:39:18,068 --> 00:39:23,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only thing you can really do that will make a difference there, it's not... not make a wrong move, because it makes no difference.

584
00:39:23,719 --> 00:39:24,921
[SPEAKER_01]: He's there to cut your head off.

585
00:39:25,282 --> 00:39:29,971
[SPEAKER_01]: He's basically abandoned that, which will be destroyed when he draws his sword.

586
00:39:29,991 --> 00:39:30,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

587
00:39:31,486 --> 00:39:34,088
[SPEAKER_01]: And that can be next second, in ten years, doesn't matter.

588
00:39:34,128 --> 00:39:34,849
[SPEAKER_01]: The sword is there.

589
00:39:34,929 --> 00:39:35,370
[SPEAKER_01]: I see it.

590
00:39:35,810 --> 00:39:37,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I still hold on to the body?

591
00:39:37,231 --> 00:39:38,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I still hold on to my emotion?

592
00:39:38,493 --> 00:39:39,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I still attached to my life?

593
00:39:39,894 --> 00:39:41,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I still delighting?

594
00:39:41,215 --> 00:39:43,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Abandon that before the sword comes.

595
00:39:43,598 --> 00:39:49,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, okay, touching, touching, moving, moving, sitting, sitting, laying, laying, second, one, two, three, four.

596
00:39:50,244 --> 00:39:51,185
[SPEAKER_01]: You're wasting your time.

597
00:39:51,465 --> 00:39:52,206
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the problem.

598
00:39:52,226 --> 00:39:54,488
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't save this body from being chopped.

599
00:39:55,149 --> 00:39:56,089
[SPEAKER_01]: By the executioner.

600
00:39:56,529 --> 00:39:59,090
[SPEAKER_01]: You can abandon it before it's chopped.

601
00:39:59,150 --> 00:40:01,231
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't abandon it by focusing on it.

602
00:40:01,271 --> 00:40:04,972
[SPEAKER_01]: You're abandoning it by removing the ownership that underlines it.

603
00:40:04,992 --> 00:40:05,772
[SPEAKER_01]: It's underneath it.

604
00:40:09,473 --> 00:40:11,173
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't remove the executioner.

605
00:40:11,353 --> 00:40:12,734
[SPEAKER_01]: You... it's inconceivable.

606
00:40:12,854 --> 00:40:16,275
[SPEAKER_01]: So that whole, the famous sutra, you would be as careful, like, not to spill.

607
00:40:17,215 --> 00:40:19,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Not to spill the water, because you'll cut the head off.

608
00:40:19,677 --> 00:40:20,437
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a different story.

609
00:40:20,457 --> 00:40:31,005
[SPEAKER_01]: But you would be as careful in the sense of, ah, I will not give in to delight, even to the point of spilling slightly towards it, because then I'm subjected to being killed.

610
00:40:32,687 --> 00:40:39,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like I will freeze and spend the rest of my life in this catatonic stupor of meditation without making a slightest motion, because

611
00:40:41,310 --> 00:40:48,075
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to recognize that that assassin, in that other certainty, who lives like your best friend, inside of you, that's you, basically.

612
00:40:48,175 --> 00:40:50,777
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your own assassin, because you remain ignorant of death.

613
00:40:52,198 --> 00:40:52,898
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's inside.

614
00:40:52,918 --> 00:40:53,859
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot outrun that.

615
00:40:53,879 --> 00:40:54,780
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot escape that.

616
00:40:56,821 --> 00:41:00,264
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can only escape in the sense of, ah, get rid of it or something.

617
00:41:00,744 --> 00:41:03,426
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can only abandon that which he's about to assassinate.

618
00:41:05,900 --> 00:41:08,222
[SPEAKER_01]: And you abandon it by seeing what's subjected to it.

619
00:41:08,682 --> 00:41:09,463
[SPEAKER_01]: What is subjected to?

620
00:41:09,543 --> 00:41:11,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's subjected to execution.

621
00:41:12,466 --> 00:41:15,408
[SPEAKER_01]: This body is subject to the to the assassin.

622
00:41:16,109 --> 00:41:17,029
[SPEAKER_01]: That is his goal.

623
00:41:20,272 --> 00:41:20,873
[SPEAKER_01]: So I have a choice.

624
00:41:20,913 --> 00:41:27,958
[SPEAKER_01]: Either ignore that and try to make the most of it, or abandon that which will be killed.

625
00:41:27,999 --> 00:41:33,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Remove my ownership of it, through seeing the inherent danger and actually impossibility to own.

626
00:41:33,183 --> 00:41:34,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if I'm true owner,

627
00:41:36,238 --> 00:41:39,959
[SPEAKER_01]: If I'm truly in control of this life and this body, no assassin can come and kill me.

628
00:41:41,219 --> 00:41:47,980
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that I am subject to death, means I'm subject to... my control is fundamentally subjected to non-control.

629
00:41:49,480 --> 00:41:55,121
[SPEAKER_01]: So there's really no choice about it, in other words.

630
00:41:55,161 --> 00:41:57,022
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, oh yeah, but I don't mind the execution.

631
00:41:57,062 --> 00:41:57,442
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you do.

632
00:41:57,682 --> 00:41:59,202
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you value life.

633
00:41:59,282 --> 00:42:00,082
[SPEAKER_01]: You value ownership.

634
00:42:00,882 --> 00:42:02,602
[SPEAKER_01]: And this is completely opposite of that.

635
00:42:03,163 --> 00:42:05,183
[SPEAKER_01]: To be taking away, ripping it away from you.

636
00:42:06,255 --> 00:42:13,620
[SPEAKER_01]: So the only thing, the only reason you think you don't mind is because you're so intoxicated with your ownership, you don't even acknowledge the threat of a drawn sword.

637
00:42:14,901 --> 00:42:16,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, but no, he can't get me.

638
00:42:17,363 --> 00:42:18,304
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, get you.

639
00:42:18,924 --> 00:42:20,465
[SPEAKER_01]: But then it's too late for you to do anything about it.

640
00:42:24,568 --> 00:42:29,092
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you see the reason, you don't become afraid and...

641
00:42:29,847 --> 00:42:34,776
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you will probably be afraid initially, because you don't really know where the problem is, and what can I do, what can I not do.

642
00:42:35,117 --> 00:42:39,324
[SPEAKER_01]: But the clearer you are about the practice, the less and less afraid you are, because you realize, well, this is inevitable.

643
00:42:40,291 --> 00:42:48,257
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, it's not about me trying to control the uncontrollable, like body movement, never move, and only eat a little bit.

644
00:42:48,517 --> 00:42:48,677
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

645
00:42:49,158 --> 00:42:51,239
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about not delighting when I delight.

646
00:42:52,060 --> 00:42:55,963
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just remove delight, remove greed, aversion, distraction.

647
00:42:56,083 --> 00:42:56,483
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

648
00:42:57,023 --> 00:43:01,147
[SPEAKER_01]: You have closed the gates through which assassin and the executioner can come and kill you.

649
00:43:05,230 --> 00:43:06,430
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, that's the true safety.

650
00:43:06,450 --> 00:43:08,052
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the other sutra that...

651
00:43:08,963 --> 00:43:12,126
[SPEAKER_01]: The monk was going around the forest saying, oh, this is so blissful, it's so blissful.

652
00:43:12,786 --> 00:43:19,531
[SPEAKER_01]: And people thought, oh, he must be thinking about his previous former wives or whatever, because he's... so they're like, he's not happy with the monk's life.

653
00:43:19,952 --> 00:43:22,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Then they called... they told the Buddha, they were to call him up.

654
00:43:22,374 --> 00:43:24,716
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, what are you referring to when you say so blissful?

655
00:43:24,756 --> 00:43:25,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it your previous lay life?

656
00:43:25,876 --> 00:43:26,297
[SPEAKER_01]: He's like, what?

657
00:43:26,357 --> 00:43:27,057
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no, no, no.

658
00:43:27,838 --> 00:43:28,778
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the opposite.

659
00:43:28,858 --> 00:43:36,900
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, when I was a layman, he was a prince, constantly in fear of his life, by his own family, by other kings, whatever, because he was next in line to the throne.

660
00:43:37,401 --> 00:43:38,701
[SPEAKER_01]: So he never had a moment of peace.

661
00:43:38,761 --> 00:43:40,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Wherever he was, he had to get guards with him.

662
00:43:41,422 --> 00:43:43,542
[SPEAKER_01]: His palaces were guarded inside and outside.

663
00:43:43,622 --> 00:43:45,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Constantly, he was constantly living in fear that he'll be killed.

664
00:43:46,383 --> 00:43:52,725
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, and now, after practice dhamma, and obviously fulfilled it, I'm living in this utter, complete,

665
00:43:53,605 --> 00:43:58,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Wilderness of a jungle with tigers and elephants and, like, deadly things.

666
00:43:58,529 --> 00:44:01,731
[SPEAKER_01]: My life is much more at risk now than it used to be.

667
00:44:02,071 --> 00:44:05,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, I feel complete ease and peace and not traces of fear.

668
00:44:06,114 --> 00:44:06,454
[SPEAKER_01]: And that...

669
00:44:06,755 --> 00:44:11,398
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when he remembers the previous life, he sees that disparity.

670
00:44:11,478 --> 00:44:12,999
[SPEAKER_01]: How much worse off he was before.

671
00:44:13,720 --> 00:44:18,123
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's just delightful for him to remember, like, how long he has come.

672
00:44:19,622 --> 00:44:20,202
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the thing.

673
00:44:20,222 --> 00:44:22,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, with all the protection in the world, no.

674
00:44:22,644 --> 00:44:28,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the reason for you being subjected to fear and danger is internally, because you delight in things that are subjected to fear and danger.

675
00:44:29,147 --> 00:44:30,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because there is fear and danger.

676
00:44:31,329 --> 00:44:31,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

677
00:44:32,689 --> 00:44:34,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, and no protection needed.

678
00:44:34,811 --> 00:44:36,732
[SPEAKER_01]: No protection needed, because there's nothing to... That's what it's like.

679
00:44:36,752 --> 00:44:41,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever you... Like when we said in the previous recording, leading up to this,

680
00:44:43,526 --> 00:44:48,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you never, like, dare to leave the monastery or home, whichever way, because of the safety.

681
00:44:48,347 --> 00:44:51,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Look, if there is a need for safety, it means you're protecting something.

682
00:44:54,008 --> 00:44:57,109
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're protecting something, it means you have taken up the ownership of that.

683
00:45:01,950 --> 00:45:04,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means there's dhamma missing there.

684
00:45:06,330 --> 00:45:07,611
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's definitely pressure.

685
00:45:07,951 --> 00:45:08,791
[SPEAKER_00]: There's pressure there.

686
00:45:11,478 --> 00:45:11,998
[SPEAKER_00]: There's dukkha.

687
00:45:12,238 --> 00:45:13,159
[SPEAKER_00]: You're subject to that.

688
00:45:22,582 --> 00:45:24,663
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, the last perception.

689
00:45:26,924 --> 00:45:33,247
[SPEAKER_00]: When a bhikkhu becomes accustomed to the perception of anatta, in what is dukkha,

690
00:45:34,125 --> 00:45:42,890
[SPEAKER_00]: His mind is devoid of eye-making, mind-making and conceit regarding this conscious body and all external objects.

691
00:45:43,450 --> 00:45:46,912
[SPEAKER_00]: It has overcome all measurements due to conceiving.

692
00:45:47,512 --> 00:45:47,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

693
00:45:48,053 --> 00:45:49,914
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what I'm saying.

694
00:45:50,034 --> 00:45:51,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Measurement due to conceiving is that.

695
00:45:52,335 --> 00:45:53,736
[SPEAKER_01]: To the extent you conceived,

696
00:45:55,114 --> 00:46:01,055
[SPEAKER_01]: To that measure, you are subject to losing, needing to protect it, safety, fear.

697
00:46:01,916 --> 00:46:05,296
[SPEAKER_01]: To the extent you developed your ownership, to that extent you'll need to defend it.

698
00:46:06,277 --> 00:46:11,538
[SPEAKER_01]: To that measure, you are subject to all the things that what you've appropriated is subjected to.

699
00:46:11,738 --> 00:46:14,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Change, discomfort and so on.

700
00:46:15,379 --> 00:46:19,099
[SPEAKER_01]: So you remove your conceivings, there's nothing you can be measured by.

701
00:46:24,972 --> 00:46:37,736
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why seeing anicca correctly, subject to change, is experienced, felt unpleasantly, which is then lived as not mine, not myself, as an atta.

702
00:46:39,897 --> 00:46:47,639
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the necessary basis for atta, for sense of ownership, and I am, I am the first, everything else is second for me,

703
00:46:49,573 --> 00:46:55,857
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not seeing that that I am is subject to change, which is uncomfortable, which is not mine.

704
00:46:58,518 --> 00:47:01,760
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are not yours, Putujana is not his, Arahant is not his.

705
00:47:02,040 --> 00:47:03,921
[SPEAKER_01]: But Putujana thinks he is, while he's not his.

706
00:47:04,361 --> 00:47:08,804
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Putujana is liable to suffering and everything else, because he lives the discrepancy of it.

707
00:47:09,344 --> 00:47:10,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Arahant doesn't think he is.

708
00:47:12,368 --> 00:47:13,549
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not his.

709
00:47:13,929 --> 00:47:16,511
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you're able to free yourself from ownership.

710
00:47:16,531 --> 00:47:19,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Because your ownership was just a misconception from the start.

711
00:47:20,254 --> 00:47:21,375
[SPEAKER_01]: You never really owned things.

712
00:47:23,176 --> 00:47:24,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, that is the start.

713
00:47:24,237 --> 00:47:25,918
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, ignorance is beginningless.

714
00:47:25,958 --> 00:47:28,500
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no point where you made a mistake and became the owner.

715
00:47:30,434 --> 00:47:31,895
[SPEAKER_01]: So, ownership is the beginningless.

716
00:47:31,975 --> 00:47:32,736
[SPEAKER_01]: It shouldn't be there.

717
00:47:32,836 --> 00:47:37,280
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot own things, but not knowing that, that's how you own things.

718
00:47:38,000 --> 00:47:41,223
[SPEAKER_01]: And that not-knowledge of ownership is beginningless.

719
00:47:41,563 --> 00:47:42,844
[SPEAKER_01]: That's saṃsara, that's avijja.

720
00:47:43,264 --> 00:47:50,490
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's why, once you understand that ownership is impossible, you cannot revert back to ownership.

721
00:47:50,750 --> 00:47:54,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing can happen that can revert you back from an arahant.

722
00:47:57,953 --> 00:48:05,581
[SPEAKER_01]: It's irreversible because you're discerning what's there, basically, on account of which you used to mistake the ownership.

723
00:48:05,601 --> 00:48:10,146
[SPEAKER_01]: So now the mistake is uprooted and you cannot possibly engage with it anymore.

724
00:48:10,286 --> 00:48:14,410
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't knowingly, through wisdom, develop ignorance.

725
00:48:14,851 --> 00:48:15,892
[SPEAKER_01]: It's mutually exclusive.

726
00:48:24,613 --> 00:48:26,073
[SPEAKER_00]: So, developing that perception.

727
00:48:26,814 --> 00:48:27,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

728
00:48:27,194 --> 00:48:29,575
[SPEAKER_00]: So, developing the perception of another.

729
00:48:30,815 --> 00:48:36,898
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said, not me, not mine, not I am.

730
00:48:38,059 --> 00:48:42,161
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the contemplation that Sotāpanna should be cultivating, whatever he experiences.

731
00:48:44,362 --> 00:48:46,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Present, past, future, feeling, perception, doesn't matter.

732
00:48:47,203 --> 00:48:48,884
[SPEAKER_01]: Not me, not for me, not I am.

733
00:48:50,945 --> 00:48:51,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Not mine, basically.

734
00:48:55,075 --> 00:48:56,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Reminding himself of what he understood.

735
00:48:57,476 --> 00:48:58,196
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, not mine.

736
00:48:58,337 --> 00:49:04,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Reminds me of the impossibility of that thing to be on in the first place.

737
00:49:04,159 --> 00:49:06,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're just keeping that position.

738
00:49:06,100 --> 00:49:11,843
[SPEAKER_01]: So, contemplating anatta correctly, re-reveals anicca and dukkha.

739
00:49:12,023 --> 00:49:12,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, just...

740
00:49:14,164 --> 00:49:20,507
[SPEAKER_01]: Contemplating anicca correctly, you will feel dukkha, you will experience anatta automatically, simultaneously.

741
00:49:21,546 --> 00:49:22,407
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one, two, three.

